/* Track: Defaults and automation. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * This is the strongest evidence in the whole corpus and it is the reason the
 * site has the shape it has. Nothing else in personal finance has effect sizes
 * like these, and nothing else works while you are not thinking about it. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "defaults-education-versus-default",
  track: "defaults", level: "judgement",
  title: "Learning about money moves your behaviour roughly a tenth as much as moving one default does",
  source: "Fernandes, Lynch and Netemeyer, Financial Literacy, Financial Education, and Downstream Financial Behaviors (Management Science, 2014), against Kaiser, Lusardi, Menkhoff and Urban, Financial Education Affects Financial Knowledge and Downstream Behaviors (Journal of Financial Economics, 2022)",
  gateIntro: "You have decided this is the year you get on top of it. There is a personal finance book on the bedside table, two podcasts queued, and a spreadsheet you built in January and have opened four times since. Your pay still lands in the everyday account and sits there until you happen to look. It is Tuesday night, you have a free hour, and you were about to spend it on chapter six.",
  gatePrompt: "You have one hour. Write what you would do with it that is not reading, as an instruction that would still be running in March.",
  evidence: "Two meta-analyses that disagree, and the disagreement is the useful part. Fernandes, Lynch and Netemeyer pooled 168 papers and found that interventions to improve financial literacy accounted for roughly a tenth of one percent of the variance in later financial behaviour, with effects decaying toward zero inside twenty months and weakest in the low-income samples that most need them. Kaiser, Lusardi, Menkhoff and Urban restricted the pool to 76 randomised trials and recovered a real effect on behaviour of around a tenth of a standard deviation, arguing the earlier null came from admitting correlational designs. Both are syntheses of other people's work rather than fresh trials, and neither runs a head-to-head against a default change.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Financial education has a small real effect on what you do, and a default change has a large one, so the hour you were about to spend learning is better spent installing.",
  why: "Take the more generous of the two meta-analyses at face value. A tenth of a standard deviation is a genuine effect and it is not nothing. Now put it beside what the default literature routinely reports for a single administrative change: participation in a retirement plan going from the high thirties to the mid eighties in percentage points, contribution rates tripling over three years, pass-through to total savings of eighty-five cents in the dollar. Those are not tenths of a standard deviation. They are step changes, and they arrive without anybody understanding anything.\n\nThe reason is that education has to survive a chain of steps and a default does not. To work, learning must be retained, recalled at the right moment, converted into an intention, and then executed against whatever else is competing for that Sunday afternoon. Every link in that chain leaks, and the decay curve in the 2014 meta-analysis is exactly what a leaking chain looks like. A default has no chain. It executes because the system executes it, and the only thing it needs from you is that you do not switch it off.\n\nThat argument does not make reading worthless. It makes reading the wrong unit of effort. What you actually want from the hour is one irreversible-ish administrative act.",
  failureMode: "Three years of podcasts, a genuinely good grasp of index funds and franking credits, and a savings rate that has not moved because nothing was ever set up. The knowledge is real and it is sitting in the wrong place. Meanwhile a colleague who could not define a management expense ratio has been contributing eleven percent since their first week because their employer enrolled them that way.",
  worked: "The instruction is boring, which is the point:\n\n\"Set up an automatic transfer of $400 from my everyday account to my savings account, dated the day after each payday, starting this pay cycle.\"\n\nNote what is not in it. No amount chosen by optimisation, no target, no review date, no conditions. The amount is deliberately one you are confident survives a bad month, because an instruction you cancel in week three is worth less than a smaller one that runs for a decade. If you want it larger later, that is a separate instruction on a separate day.\n\nThe honest version of tonight's hour is roughly eleven minutes of banking app and forty-nine minutes back.",
  experiment: "Open your banking app now, before you read anything else today. Set one automatic transfer, dated the day after your next pay lands, for an amount small enough that you are certain it will not bounce. Then write down the date you set it up and the date you expect to notice it. Most people cannot remember, three months later, what the amount was, which is the evidence that it worked.",
  reflection: "What did you install, and how long had you known you should?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the transfer I set up today still be running, at the same amount or higher, in ninety days?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Financial education has a measurable effect on behaviour, so why is it the wrong place to spend effort?",
    a: "Because the effect is roughly a tenth of a standard deviation and it decays within about twenty months, while a single default change routinely produces step changes in participation and contribution rates. Education has to be retained, recalled at the right moment, turned into an intention and then executed, and every one of those links leaks. A default executes without you.\n\nThe two meta-analyses disagree about whether the education effect is near zero or merely small. They do not disagree about the order of magnitude between it and a default."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is what I currently know about my finances and what I have actually set up. Tell me which single administrative change would move the most, and stop me from adding a fifth thing to read."
},
{
  id: "defaults-opt-out-enrolment",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "Switching enrolment from opt-in to opt-out roughly doubles participation, and nobody has to be persuaded of anything",
  source: "Brigitte Madrian and Dennis Shea, The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2001)",
  gateIntro: "A friend has been at their job eight months and has never opened the benefits portal. They are not careless. They have a mortgage application in progress, a toddler, and a login they would have to reset. There is an employer match sitting behind that login which they have never claimed, and they know it, and they have known it since week two. They ask you what they should do about it, in the pub, on a Friday.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction you would give them, in one sentence they could act on from their phone before the next round arrives.",
  evidence: "A single large United States corporation that changed its 401(k) from opt-in to automatic enrolment, compared across hire cohorts either side of the switch. Participation among new hires was roughly in the high thirties in percentage points before and roughly in the mid eighties after. The second finding was the more uncomfortable one: most automatically enrolled employees stayed at both the default contribution rate and the default fund, neither of which had been chosen for them on any grounds of suitability. One firm, one plan, no randomisation, and the cohorts differ in when they were hired. The class of finding has since been reproduced across many plans and in the United Kingdom's national rollout, which is what lifts it above a single-site curiosity.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Enrolment framing is worth more than persuasion: opt-out roughly doubles participation, and most people never leave the default rate or the default fund.",
  idea: "Whether you participate is determined mostly by which way the form points, so the highest-leverage thing you can do to yourself is arrange for the passive path to be the one you would have chosen.",
  why: "The uncomfortable reading of Madrian and Shea is not that people are lazy. It is that participation was never really a decision. Under opt-in, doing nothing produced non-participation; under opt-out, doing nothing produced participation; and in both regimes the great majority did nothing. The preference that economics would like to infer from the choice was not doing the work. The form was.\n\nThe stickiness finding is what makes this practical rather than merely interesting. Employees stayed at the default contribution rate and in the default fund, which in the plan studied was a money market fund, a place almost nobody would choose for a thirty-year horizon if asked directly. So the default did not just determine whether people saved. It determined how much and into what, for years, silently.\n\nTurn that around and it becomes a tool. You cannot make yourself attentive, but you can spend one attentive hour arranging the passive path, and then collect the compounding benefit of your own future inattention. That is the entire method of this site in one finding.",
  failureMode: "You leave the employer match unclaimed for two years because claiming it requires a portal login you keep meaning to reset. The cost is not the fee or the market. It is a bounded, known, free amount of money that you declined by not filling in a form, and no amount of understanding compound interest closes that gap.",
  worked: "One sentence, and it has to be doable from a phone in a pub:\n\n\"Reset your benefits portal password now, while we are sitting here, and set your contribution to whatever gets the full match.\"\n\nThen the second half, which is the part people skip:\n\n\"While you are in there, check what fund it went into by default. If it is cash or a capital-stable option and you are thirty-four, change it once, today, and then never look again.\"\n\nThe two acts are different in kind. The first claims money that is being offered. The second undoes a default that was set by somebody who did not know your horizon.",
  experiment: "Find out, today, what your employer contribution arrangement actually is and whether you are capturing all of it, and separately what investment option your balance is sitting in. Both answers are usually two clicks apart in the same portal. Write both down. If either one turns out to be a default you never chose, you have just found the cheapest change available to you this year.",
  reflection: "What was the default you had never inspected, and had it been set for years?",
  recall: {
    q: "What did Madrian and Shea find beyond the change in participation rate, and why does that second finding matter more for someone acting on it?",
    a: "That most automatically enrolled employees remained at both the default contribution rate and the default investment fund, which in that plan was a money market fund. So the default determined not only whether people saved but how much and into what, for years.\n\nIt matters more because it means arranging your own defaults well is not a one-off nudge to get started. It sets the amount and the allocation you will actually live with, and those persist through exactly the inattention that made the default bind in the first place."
  },
  deepDive: "Walk me through every default currently operating on my money that I did not personally choose, and tell me which one is costing the most."
},
{
  id: "defaults-escalate-with-raises",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "Committing future pay rises to saving beats committing current income, because the loss never arrives",
  source: "Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi, Save More Tomorrow: Using Behavioral Economics to Increase Employee Saving (Journal of Political Economy, 2004)",
  gateIntro: "Your review is in six weeks and you are reasonably confident of something between three and five percent. Last year you got four, and by August you could not have said where it went. Rent took some of it. The rest is not identifiable. You have a vague intention to do better with this one, which is exactly the intention you had last year at the same point in the cycle.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that captures the raise before you meet it, including who you have to tell and by when.",
  evidence: "A mid-sized United States manufacturing firm where employees who met a financial adviser were offered a plan that raised their contribution rate automatically at each future pay rise. Those who joined moved from a savings rate of roughly three and a half percent to roughly thirteen and a half percent over about forty months and four scheduled increases, and most stayed enrolled through all of them. Not randomised, and self-selected: the people who agreed to the escalation plan were the people who had already agreed to sit with an adviser. The escalation mechanism has since been adopted very widely and its effect on contribution rates has been observed repeatedly, which is the basis for treating the mechanism rather than this firm's numbers as established.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Bind the raise before it lands: escalating contributions at each pay rise took one firm from three and a half percent to thirteen and a half over forty months.",
  idea: "Schedule the increase to coincide with the pay rise, so the saving rises without take-home pay ever falling.",
  why: "Two things make this work and they are both about what the increase feels like rather than what it costs. The first is that a cut to spending you have not started doing yet is nearly painless, while a cut to spending you are doing now is a loss, and losses are felt roughly twice as hard as equivalent gains. Deferring the commitment to a future self who has more money moves the whole decision out of the loss domain.\n\nThe second is that nominal take-home pay never goes down. If the contribution rises by two points on the same day gross pay rises by four, the number that hits the account still increases, and the reference point that spending habits are anchored to never gets violated. Nothing has to be given up, which means there is no month in which the plan feels like a sacrifice and therefore no month in which cancelling it feels like relief.\n\nThe mechanism generalises past employer plans. Any recurring transfer you own can carry a scheduled step, and the step can be pinned to the date your pay changes rather than to a date you picked.",
  failureMode: "You get four percent and resolve to save most of it. Nothing is scheduled, so the money lands in the everyday account and joins the general pool, and within two pay cycles your baseline spending has quietly absorbed it. Twelve months later your savings rate is where it was and your income is higher, which is the most common way a good year produces no wealth at all.",
  worked: "Two instructions, and the second is the one that does the work:\n\n\"Increase the automatic transfer from $400 to $500 per pay, effective the first pay cycle after my review takes effect.\"\n\n\"Put a calendar entry on the day the new pay rate first lands, titled 'raise the transfer', with the new amount already written in it.\"\n\nIf your employer plan supports automatic escalation, use that instead and skip the calendar entry entirely, because the version that does not need you is strictly better than the version that reminds you. The calendar entry is the fallback for a system that has no escalation feature, and it should name the amount so that future you does not have to decide anything.",
  experiment: "Work out the date your next pay change actually lands, not the date of the review. Then either switch on automatic escalation in your employer plan, or put a single calendar entry on that date with the new transfer amount written into the title. One of those two, today, while the number is still hypothetical and therefore cheap to commit.",
  reflection: "What happened to your last pay rise, specifically, and could you name where it went?",
  forecast: { q: "Will my next pay rise be captured by a scheduled increase rather than absorbed into everyday spending?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why does binding a future pay rise to saving work better than committing the same amount from current income?",
    a: "Because a reduction in spending you have not yet begun is not experienced as a loss, whereas cutting current spending is, and losses weigh roughly twice as heavily as equivalent gains. Deferring the commitment moves it out of the loss domain.\n\nAnd because nominal take-home pay never falls: if the contribution rises on the same day gross pay rises, the amount landing in the account still goes up, so the plan never has a month that feels like sacrifice and therefore never has a month when cancelling it feels like relief."
  },
  deepDive: "My next pay review is coming. Help me set the escalation amount so that it is aggressive enough to matter and small enough that I will not cancel it in month three."
},
{
  id: "defaults-mandates-beat-incentives",
  track: "defaults", level: "judgement",
  title: "Money moved automatically mostly stays saved, money moved by a tax break mostly comes from savings you already had",
  source: "Raj Chetty, John Friedman, Soren Leth-Petersen, Torben Heien Nielsen and Tore Olsen, Active vs. Passive Decisions and Crowd-Out in Retirement Savings Accounts: Evidence from Denmark (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2014)",
  gateIntro: "A colleague is arguing that you should be salary sacrificing right up to the cap because of the tax saving, and the arithmetic they are showing you on the whiteboard is correct. You notice you have heard this argument three times and never acted on it, and that the reason is not that you doubt the tax rates. It is that the money would have to come from somewhere and you have never worked out where.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that answers where the money comes from, rather than the calculation that shows why it should.",
  evidence: "Danish administrative tax and wealth records covering tens of millions of person-year observations, using policy changes and job switches to separate two channels. Contributions that happened automatically, through mandatory or employer-driven arrangements, passed through to total household saving at around eighty-five cents in the dollar. Price subsidies delivered through tax deductions generated on the order of one cent of new saving per dollar of forgone government revenue, because the people who responded were disproportionately those who simply relocated existing savings. Around eighty-five percent of the population behaved passively, not responding to the subsidy at all. One country, one set of institutions, one period, and the identification rests on the specific Danish reforms, so the magnitudes should not be exported wholesale.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "A tax incentive changes where your existing savings sit, an automatic contribution changes how much you save, and only one of those makes you wealthier.",
  why: "The Danish result splits a question that usually gets asked as one. Ask whether a tax-advantaged account raises national saving and the answer depends entirely on which channel is doing the work. If people respond to the tax break by shifting a term deposit into the sheltered account, total saving is unchanged and the government has bought a reshuffle. If the contribution happens automatically out of gross pay, the money was never available to shift, so it is new.\n\nThe eighty-five percent passive share is the load-bearing number. It says that for most people the subsidy is not an input at all, because they are not running the optimisation the subsidy is priced for. They are not weighing marginal rates. They are doing nothing, and what determines their saving is what happens when they do nothing.\n\nWhich means the correct personal reading is almost the opposite of the whiteboard argument. The tax break is real and worth having, but it is not the mechanism. The mechanism is that the contribution leaves before you see it. If you want to be in the fifteen percent that the subsidy reaches, fine, but the reliable move is to make yourself one of the eighty-five for whom the automatic path is the good one.",
  failureMode: "You are persuaded by the tax arithmetic, make a large voluntary contribution in June out of your offset account, and feel like you have saved. Your total assets have not changed. You have relocated money and paid an entry price in liquidity, and next June there is nothing left in the offset to relocate, so the strategy quietly stops.",
  worked: "The instruction has to name the source, not the destination:\n\n\"Set up a salary sacrifice of $300 per pay from gross salary into super, starting next cycle, so the money never reaches my everyday account.\"\n\nThat is different in kind from:\n\n\"Make a $7,800 personal contribution in June and claim the deduction.\"\n\nBoth end the year with the same balance and the same deduction. Only the first one is saving. The second is a transfer, and it depends on there being a pile to transfer from, which is precisely the thing you were trying to build.",
  experiment: "Look at your last voluntary contribution or investment purchase, if you made one, and answer where the money came from. If it came out of an existing balance, it was a relocation and your wealth did not change that day. Then set up one recurring contribution that comes out of gross or out of pay on arrival, at any amount, so that at least one channel is genuinely additive.",
  reflection: "Of the money you moved into investments last year, how much was new saving and how much was relocation?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I be able to show, twelve months from now, that my total household assets rose by more than the amount I relocated between accounts?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why did tax subsidies for retirement saving in Denmark generate roughly a cent of new saving per dollar spent, while automatic contributions passed through at around eighty-five cents?",
    a: "Because the subsidy was mostly claimed by people relocating savings they already had, so total saving barely moved, while automatic contributions came out of income before it was available to shift and were therefore additive.\n\nAround eighty-five percent of the population was passive and did not respond to the price incentive at all, which is why a mechanism aimed at optimisers reached so few people while a mechanism that operates on inattention reached nearly everyone."
  },
  deepDive: "Separate my last year of investment activity into new saving and relocation, and tell me what my actual savings rate was rather than what my contribution total suggests."
},
{
  id: "defaults-superannuation-guarantee",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "Australia legislated the default this literature recommends, and it is currently moving twelve percent of your wage",
  source: "Superannuation Guarantee (Administration) Act 1992, and the legislated rate schedule reaching 12 percent from 1 July 2025",
  gateIntro: "You have never once set up a retirement contribution and you have a retirement balance in six figures. Somebody at a barbecue is explaining that Australians are terrible savers, and you are half agreeing, while privately aware that the only reason you have any long-term assets at all is a payroll line item you have never looked at and could not describe the mechanics of.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that would tell you, once a year, whether the legislated contribution is actually arriving and where it is going.",
  evidence: "Statute rather than a finding. The Superannuation Guarantee obliges employers to contribute a legislated percentage of ordinary time earnings to a complying fund, and the schedule of increases written into law reached 12 percent on 1 July 2025. It is a mandatory, employer-side, opt-out-by-nobody contribution, which makes it the largest-scale live instance of exactly the mechanism the defaults literature identifies. It is legislation, so it can be amended, and the rate, the earnings base and the associated caps have all moved before.",
  replication: "statute",
  asAt: "2026-09-02",
  sourceUrl: "https://www.ato.gov.au/tax-rates-and-codes/key-superannuation-rates-and-thresholds",
  verifiedOn: "2026-09-02",
  cheat: "The Superannuation Guarantee rate is 12 percent of ordinary time earnings from 1 July 2025, contributed by your employer whether or not you ever act.",
  idea: "The single largest wealth-building instruction operating on you was installed by Parliament rather than by you, which tells you what the mechanism is worth and also what it is missing.",
  why: "Look at what the Guarantee gets right by design. It is compulsory, so participation is universal. It comes from the employer side out of gross pay, so it is additive rather than a relocation. It is a percentage, so it escalates automatically with every pay rise you ever get, without a decision. That is opt-out enrolment, automatic escalation and gross-pay sourcing, all three, running for decades on people who have never thought about it once.\n\nNow look at what it does not do. The rate is set for the median case and there is no reason to think it is right for yours. The fund is whichever default your first employer used unless you moved it, and the investment option inside that fund is whichever default the fund uses. And the Guarantee only ever touches wages, so income from anything else is outside it entirely.\n\nSo the honest position is that the state has already installed the hard part and left you three annual jobs: check it arrived, check where it went, and decide whether the legislated rate is the rate you want. Those are small, and they are the whole remaining surface.",
  failureMode: "You have four super accounts from four employers, three of them paying administration fees and one insurance premium each on balances too small to notice, and the twelve percent is landing in the account attached to your current job while the other three erode. Nothing here required a bad decision. It required nine years of not making one.",
  worked: "One recurring instruction, annual, and it checks one thing only:\n\n\"Every year in the week after 1 July, log in to the ATO portal and confirm that the Guarantee contributions for all four quarters of the year just closed actually arrived.\"\n\nThe date is doing the work. A contribution year that has just closed is a complete record, so a missing quarter is visible as a gap rather than merely late, and unpaid superannuation is common enough that this is a real check and not a formality.\n\nDeliberately not on this line: how many accounts you hold, which fund the staple points at, and whether twelve percent is the right rate for you. Those are three separate jobs with their own entries, and bundling them here is how a one-minute annual check turns into an afternoon nobody schedules.",
  experiment: "Log in to myGov, open the ATO section, and look at the contribution history for the last four quarters. You are checking one thing: that a payment appears for each quarter, at roughly the rate on your salary. A missing or short quarter is worth raising with your employer immediately, because recovery gets harder with time and the obligation is legislated rather than discretionary.",
  reflection: "Did all four quarters appear, and at roughly the rate you expected?",
  recall: {
    q: "The Superannuation Guarantee implements three separate mechanisms the defaults literature recommends. Name them, and name what it leaves to you.",
    a: "Opt-out enrolment, because it is compulsory and universal. Automatic escalation, because it is a percentage of earnings and therefore rises with every pay increase without a decision. And gross-pay sourcing, because it comes from the employer before the money reaches you, which makes it additive rather than a relocation of existing savings.\n\nWhat it leaves to you is the rate, which is set for the median case rather than yours, the fund and the investment option inside it, both of which are somebody else's defaults, and everything that is not wage income."
  },
  deepDive: "Given my age, income and current balance, tell me whether the legislated twelve percent is the right rate for me and what the gap costs if it is not."
},
{
  id: "defaults-payday-timing",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "Date the transfer to the day after pay lands, because a transfer that fails once is a transfer you switch off",
  source: "Standard practice in retail banking and payroll-linked savings design, and the operational rationale behind pay-cycle-aligned deductions",
  gateIntro: "The transfer you set up in March is scheduled for the twenty-eighth, which was a fine choice when you picked it. Your pay arrives on the first Thursday of the month. Twice now the transfer has attempted itself against a nearly empty account, once succeeding and leaving you short for four days, once failing with a fee. You have started to think of the transfer as a problem.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the corrected instruction, including the exact rule you would use to pick the date rather than the date itself.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is operational practice rather than a finding: payroll-linked savings products, employer deduction schemes and bank round-up features are all built to move money at or immediately after the moment income arrives, and the stated rationale is that a deduction competing against a depleted balance either fails or displaces something else. There is a large behavioural literature on mental accounting and on the salience of account balances that is consistent with it, but no experiment isolating transfer timing that this entry is resting on.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Put the transfer on the day after income arrives, so it never competes with a low balance and therefore never gives you a reason to cancel it.",
  why: "An automatic transfer has exactly one failure mode that matters, and it is not being too small. It is being switched off. Everything that makes cancellation feel sensible is therefore worth engineering away, and the largest single source of that feeling is a transfer that has caused you a problem.\n\nA transfer dated late in the cycle competes with whatever is left, which is by construction the smallest balance of the month. When it wins, you are short and you notice; when it loses, you pay a fee and you notice. Either way the transfer has now been the cause of a bad afternoon, and the cheapest available fix from inside that afternoon is to cancel it. A transfer dated the day after pay competes with the largest balance of the month, and the money is gone before any of the month's obligations have arrived to argue with it.\n\nThe secondary effect is that it reverses the residual. Saving what is left over makes the amount a residual of your spending. Moving it first makes your spending the residual, and a residual is what adjusts.",
  failureMode: "The transfer bounces in a tight month, costs a dishonour fee, and you cancel it that evening while annoyed. Eleven months later you remember it existed. The amount was never the problem and the scheduling was, and the cost is eleven months of contributions plus the fee.",
  worked: "The instruction should carry the rule, not the number:\n\n\"Move the automatic transfer to run one business day after each pay deposit, and if my pay cycle changes, move the transfer on the same day I find out.\"\n\nIf your bank cannot schedule relative to a deposit, take the safe approximation:\n\n\"Set the transfer to the fixed day one day after my earliest possible payday, and set the amount to what survives my worst month rather than my average one.\"\n\nThe second clause matters more than it looks. An amount sized to an average month fails in a bad one, and one failure is enough to end the arrangement.",
  experiment: "Look up the actual date each of your recurring transfers runs and the actual date your pay lands. Fix any transfer that sits in the back half of the cycle by moving it to the day after pay. While you are there, check whether any of them has ever failed, because a past failure is the best predictor you have of the arrangement not surviving the year.",
  reflection: "Which of your transfers was scheduled against the emptiest part of the month, and had it already caused a problem?",
  forecast: { q: "Will every one of my recurring transfers execute successfully for the next six consecutive pay cycles?" },
  recall: {
    q: "What is the only failure mode of an automatic transfer that really matters, and how does the scheduling date drive it?",
    a: "Being switched off. A transfer dated late in the pay cycle competes with the smallest balance of the month, so it either leaves you short or fails and incurs a fee, and in both cases it becomes the identifiable cause of a bad afternoon whose cheapest fix is cancellation.\n\nDated the day after income arrives, it competes with the largest balance instead and completes before the month's obligations exist. It also inverts the residual, so spending adjusts to what is left rather than saving getting whatever survives."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my pay dates and every recurring transfer and direct debit I have. Reschedule them so nothing ever competes with a depleted balance."
},
{
  id: "defaults-path-of-least-resistance",
  track: "defaults", level: "judgement",
  title: "Whatever your scheme makes easiest predicts your saving better than your intentions about it do",
  source: "Choi, Laibson, Madrian and Metrick, Defined Contribution Pensions: Plan Rules, Participant Choices, and the Path of Least Resistance (Tax Policy and the Economy, 2002)",
  gateIntro: "You told your partner in February that you were going to lift the contribution rate. You said it again in May, and once more over dinner in August, meaning it every time. It is now September, the payroll line has not changed, and the form that would change it takes about four minutes. Nothing has stopped you. Nothing has happened either.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that closes the gap between what you have been saying since February and what is currently running, with today's date in it.",
  evidence: "A set of large United States firms whose retirement plans differed in their administrative details, with the choices of tens of thousands of participants compared across those details. Wherever a plan supplied a default - an enrolment method, a contribution rate, an investment mix, a fund menu - the modal participant sat on it, and plans with different defaults produced different modal participants out of broadly comparable workforces. The paper also put a survey beside the administrative data: a large majority of participants said their own savings rate was too low, most of those said they intended to raise it soon, and only a small fraction had done anything about it several months later. Same research team across the whole firm sample, no randomisation anywhere in it, and these are large employers with professional workforces rather than a cross-section of the labour market.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "The administrative details of the scheme you happen to be in carry more information about your future saving than anything you sincerely believe about it.",
  why: "The title is the finding. Across firms with different plan rules, participants converged on whatever each plan made frictionless, and the differences between the workforces were smaller than the differences between the forms they were handed. That is a strange result if saving reflects a considered preference and an obvious one if it reflects a queue of small administrative acts that never reach the front.\n\nThe survey half is what makes it personal, and it is the uncomfortable half. Most participants diagnosed themselves correctly. They knew the rate was too low, they said so, they said they would fix it soon, and months later they were still saying it at the same rate. So the self-diagnosis was accurate and inert. The intention was genuine; it simply was not a cause of anything.\n\nWhat that does is disqualify your own reported plans as evidence about yourself. If you want to know what you will save next year, do not consult how you feel about saving. Look at what is scheduled, because that is the whole of it, and treat everything unscheduled as a forecast with a known track record.\n\nThe inversion is that you get to pick your own frictionless path, once. You cannot become the sort of person who fills in the form in month three, but you can spend four minutes arranging the thing that runs while you are not that person.",
  failureMode: "Seven years of correctly identifying that your contribution rate is too low. You can produce the right answer at any dinner party and you have produced it several times, and the payroll line has not moved since your second job. The diagnosis was never the bottleneck. The four-minute form was.",
  worked: "The instruction has to be dated and it has to name the act rather than the goal:\n\n\"Today, before I close this laptop, raise my salary sacrifice from $200 to $300 per pay, effective next cycle.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately missing is any version of \"and first I will work out whether $300 is the right figure\". That clause is where the last three attempts died. The figure does not have to be right. It has to be running, because a wrong number that runs beats a right number still under consideration in March.\n\nIf today is genuinely impossible, then the instruction is not the transfer. It is: \"Book thirty minutes tomorrow morning titled 'raise the sacrifice to $300' and do not move it.\"",
  experiment: "Write down what your contribution rate and your automatic transfer amounts actually are right now, read off the screens rather than from memory. Beside them, write what you have been telling yourself they should be. The gap between those two columns is the only number in this entry, and it has a date attached, which is the day you first noticed it. Close one row of the gap before you close the app.",
  reflection: "How long had you known the number was wrong before today?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the change I have been intending since February be running before the end of this week?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Participants in this study diagnosed their own under-saving accurately. Why does that make their intentions less useful as evidence rather than more?",
    a: "Because accuracy and causal force are different properties. They correctly identified that their rate was too low and said they intended to raise it, and months later the great majority were at the same rate. The diagnosis was right and did nothing, which means your own reported plans carry no information about what you will actually do.\n\nWhat did carry information was the plan's administrative detail. Across firms, the modal contribution rate, fund and enrolment status tracked whatever each plan made frictionless, so the form predicted the behaviour better than the person did."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is what I have been intending to change about my saving for the past year and here is what is actually scheduled. Tell me which single one to schedule today, and stop me from turning it back into a research task."
},
{
  id: "defaults-active-choice",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "Being made to choose beats being asked to opt in and loses to being enrolled",
  source: "Carroll, Choi, Laibson, Madrian and Metrick, Optimal Defaults and Active Decisions (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2009)",
  gateIntro: "Your team has a new starter and you are the one showing them around. Their onboarding pack has a page about the retirement plan with a box to tick if they want to join, and the pack has gone into a drawer along with the parking form and the ergonomic assessment. On the way to coffee they ask you whether any of it is actually urgent.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence you would say to them, phrased so that doing nothing is not one of the options it leaves open.",
  evidence: "A large United States firm that for a period required every new hire to make an explicit election within thirty days of starting - enrol at a rate of their choosing, or decline - with no option to simply not respond, compared against cohorts hired under the firm's ordinary opt-in regime. Participation at short tenure rose by a large margin under the active-decision rule, and it did not reach the levels the same literature reports under automatic enrolment. The paper's contribution is the theory as much as the estimate: requiring a choice is the better design where people's circumstances genuinely differ and procrastination is moderate, and a default is better where they do not. One firm, one regime change, cohorts that differ in hiring date, and no measurement at all of whether the rates people actively chose were better for them than the default would have been.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Removing the do-nothing option lifts participation a long way above opt-in without imposing anybody's number on you, which makes it the right design wherever the right answer really is person-specific.",
  why: "The three regimes differ in exactly one respect: what happens when you do not reply. Under opt-in, silence means out. Under automatic enrolment, silence means in, at a rate somebody else typed. Under a forced election, silence is not on offer, because the process will not close until you answer, so the outcome has to come out of you rather than out of the form.\n\nThat middle design is the one to reach for when the answer is genuinely yours. Automatic enrolment buys the highest participation and pays for it by installing a level nobody picked, which is fine where the median answer is close enough for almost everybody and expensive where it is not. A forced election recovers most of the participation and leaves the number with the person whose money it is. The paper frames this as a trade-off rather than a ranking, and which design wins turns on how much people actually differ and how badly they procrastinate.\n\nThe mechanism does not need an employer, which is why it is installable. Anywhere a decision of yours can be resolved by silence, silence will resolve it. You convert it into a forced question by attaching it to something that proceeds without you. A recurring reminder can be dismissed forever. A transfer that steps up next month unless you set a different figure cannot.",
  failureMode: "The new starter never opens the pack, and eight months later has no contributions, no idea what cover was bundled in, and no memory of declining anything. Nobody made a bad decision. The document permitted silence, silence is the most common answer to any form, so the document decided.",
  worked: "Two versions, and the difference between them is the entire entry.\n\nThe weak one:\n\n\"I should work out what my contribution rate ought to be.\"\n\nThe forced one:\n\n\"On the first of next month my transfer goes from $400 to $600 unless I have set a different figure before then, and the increase is already scheduled.\"\n\nThe second is a question you cannot decline, because declining it now requires an act. Note that the pre-set figure is not a recommendation and is not trying to be right. It exists so that not choosing has a cost, which is the only property that reliably gets a question answered.",
  experiment: "Find one money decision you have left open for more than a month: the contribution rate, the insurance inside the fund, offset versus redraw, the fund itself. Convert it today into something that resolves without you, by scheduling the change at a figure you would tolerate, with a date. From this afternoon you have to either accept it or overrule it, and both of those are answers.",
  reflection: "Which open question did you convert into a scheduled change, and how long had it been open?",
  recall: {
    q: "Automatic enrolment produces higher participation than forcing a choice. Why would you ever prefer the forced choice?",
    a: "Because the two designs pay different prices. Automatic enrolment buys the highest participation by installing a rate and an allocation that nobody chose, which is cheap where the median answer suits almost everybody and expensive where circumstances genuinely differ. A forced election recovers most of the participation gain and leaves the number with the person whose money it is.\n\nCarroll, Choi, Laibson, Madrian and Metrick present it as a trade-off rather than a ranking: the default wins where preferences are similar and procrastination is severe, the forced choice wins where preferences vary and procrastination is moderate. Their firm's thirty-day election rule lifted participation at short tenure well above opt-in and stayed below what automatic enrolment reaches."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the money decisions I have been leaving open. For each one tell me whether to install a default or force myself to choose, and give me the mechanism that makes the choice unavoidable."
},
{
  id: "defaults-rate-anchoring",
  track: "defaults", level: "annual",
  title: "A default does not just start you, it sets your number, and a low one will hold you there for years",
  source: "Beshears, Choi, Laibson and Madrian on the limitations of defaults, read against the contribution-rate distributions reported across automatically enrolled plans",
  gateIntro: "Your first job out of university signed you up at three percent, because three was the figure printed on the form, and you have since changed employers twice, doubled your income and never touched it. Someone who started the same year at a different firm went in at nine, has equally never touched it, and now has roughly twice your balance. Neither of you decided anything.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that sets the rate to a figure you picked rather than a figure a form picked, including the date it takes effect.",
  evidence: "The stickiness half of the automatic enrolment literature rather than the participation half, and it has been observed again and again. Across plans that adopted automatic enrolment, the distribution of contribution rates develops a large spike exactly at whatever the plan's default rate is, and the spike persists across years of tenure rather than dissolving as people settle in. Beshears, Choi, Laibson and Madrian draw the consequence in their work on the limitations of defaults: because a default anchors rather than merely starting people, one set low can leave people saving less than they would have chosen under a regime that made them decide. The clustering is about as well established as anything in this field. The counterfactual is much weaker, because what a particular person would have chosen in the absence of a default is never observed, and the plans that choose low defaults are not a random sample of plans.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Pick your own contribution rate in the week you are enrolled: wherever a plan sets a default rate, the distribution piles up on that rate and stays piled up for years.",
  idea: "The default contribution rate becomes the modal contribution rate and stays there, so an enrolment default decides your saving level and not merely whether you save.",
  why: "Automatic enrolment usually gets discussed as though its only job were getting people through the door, with the level sorting itself out afterwards. The rate distributions say otherwise. They show a spike at the default that is still there years into tenure, which means the door and the level were one decision, taken once, by whoever typed the figure into the plan document.\n\nThe mechanism is the same inertia that produced the participation result, pointed at a different variable. Changing your rate requires the identical sequence of small administrative acts that enrolling required, so it fails for the identical reason, and there is no moment at which the system asks you again. A default is therefore not a starting position you drift away from. It is a resting position you stay at, and its power is symmetric: set high it saves people who would not have acted, set low it stops people who would have.\n\nThis is the honest limit of the whole case for defaults, and it is why the useful move is installing your own rather than admiring somebody else's. The evidence that defaults determine behaviour is exactly the evidence that a badly chosen default determines behaviour. Somebody set the one currently operating on you, and they were not thinking about you when they set it.",
  failureMode: "Twelve years at the rate printed on your first onboarding form, through two employers and a doubled salary, because the rate travelled with you as a percentage and the percentage was never yours. The cost is not a fee and not a market. It is a decade of contributions at a level chosen by a plan administrator you never met.",
  worked: "The instruction is annual and it has one job, which is to make the rate something you pick:\n\n\"In the first week of July each year, look up the contribution rate currently running, write down the rate I would choose today if nothing were set, and if they differ by more than one percentage point, change it that afternoon.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any comparison against a benchmark or against what other people do. The comparison is between the running figure and your own answer, because the failure being engineered against is not picking the wrong rate. It is never having answered the question at all, which is what a spike at the default looks like from the inside.",
  experiment: "Find the rate that is running on you right now, in the fund and in any transfer you own, and then find out who set it. If the answer is a form you filled in years ago at a different salary, or an employer's plan document, you have located an anchor rather than a decision. Change it today by any amount, even a single percentage point, because the point of the first change is to establish that the figure moves.",
  reflection: "What rate is running on you, and who actually chose it?",
  recall: {
    q: "The evidence that defaults raise participation and the evidence that defaults can lower saving are the same evidence. Explain why.",
    a: "Because both rest on one observation: the contribution-rate distribution develops a spike at whatever the plan default is, and the spike persists for years. Inertia holds people wherever the default put them, and it holds them there symmetrically. A default above what someone would have chosen raises their saving and one below it lowers theirs, and nothing in the mechanism distinguishes the two cases.\n\nThe clustering is well established across many plans. The claim that a low default leaves people worse off than they would otherwise have been is weaker, because the rate a given person would have chosen without a default is never observed, and plans do not set their defaults at random."
  },
  deepDive: "Tell me what contribution rate I should actually be running given my age, income, balance and horizon, then tell me how far the rate currently running on me sits from it and who set that one."
},
{
  id: "defaults-national-rollout",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "The opt-out result survived being scaled to an entire country, and almost nobody used the exit",
  source: "The United Kingdom's Pensions Act 2008 automatic enrolment programme, phased in from 2012, as analysed by Cribb and Emmerson at the Institute for Fiscal Studies",
  gateIntro: "Your employer sends a benefits email in November saying that everyone will be moved onto a new default arrangement in January, and that anybody who would rather not can reply and be left out of it. You read it twice, decide you probably want to look properly at whether the new arrangement suits you, and file the email. January arrives.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that settles this now rather than in January, and say in it what happens if you never reply.",
  evidence: "A national natural experiment rather than a single firm. The Pensions Act 2008 obliged United Kingdom employers to enrol eligible workers into a workplace pension automatically from 2012, phased in by employer size over several years, with employees free to leave at any time at no cost. Institute for Fiscal Studies work by Cribb and Emmerson finds participation among eligible private-sector employees rising from around half to roughly nine in ten across the rollout, with opt-out rates staying low, on the order of one in ten, and far below what had been forecast before the programme started. Two limitations matter. Contributions concentrated heavily at the legislated minimum, which is the anchoring problem arriving at national scale. And participation is not saving: the evidence on whether total household wealth rose by the full amount of the new contributions, rather than being partly offset by other saving or by borrowing, is much thinner than the participation evidence.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Assume you will never use an opt-out you are given: the United Kingdom auto-enrolled its private-sector workforce, exit was free, and roughly one in ten took it.",
  idea: "Making the passive path good works at the scale of a national workforce, and the freedom to leave that people insist is the important safeguard turns out to be used by very few of them.",
  why: "Madrian and Shea's result came with an obvious objection attached: one firm, one workforce, one plan, possibly a peculiarity of that population. The United Kingdom answered the objection by running the same intervention on millions of people across every industry and every income band, with an exit that was free, immediate and advertised. Participation among eligible private-sector employees went from roughly half to roughly nine in ten and stayed there.\n\nThe part worth sitting with is how few left. The standard economic reading says that anybody for whom the arrangement was wrong would simply go, since going cost nothing. Very few went, which means the ease of exit was not the binding constraint on anything. What bound was that leaving took an act and staying took none.\n\nThat has a direct personal consequence and it is not flattering. Any exit you are ever offered is a right you will probably not exercise, so treat whatever you are placed into as the thing you will keep. The only reliable moment to assess an arrangement is the week it is switched on, because after that week assessing it stops being something that gets done.\n\nThe rollout also reproduced the anchoring problem faithfully. Contributions bunched at the legal minimum, which is exactly what the mechanism predicts and exactly what a country designing a national default ought to have expected.",
  failureMode: "The November email said you could decline and you decided to look into it. Eighteen months later you are still in an arrangement you never assessed, paying whatever it charges and invested however it invests, and the looking into it has never been scheduled. Nothing was imposed on you. You simply never used the exit you were keeping as an option.",
  worked: "One instruction, and it is a rule about timing rather than an amount:\n\n\"Whenever something is switched on for me by default, I assess it in the first week and then either accept it or leave, and I never carry it as an open question.\"\n\nThe concrete version for the email in the drawer:\n\n\"Before Friday, open the new arrangement, write down the fee, the investment option and the contribution rate, and either change one of them or write 'accepted' beside all three.\"\n\nThe word 'accepted' is doing real work there. An arrangement you never assessed and an arrangement you assessed and kept produce the identical balance and are completely different positions, because only one of them will ever get looked at again.",
  experiment: "List every arrangement currently running on your money that you were placed into rather than chose: the super fund, the investment option inside it, the insurance attached to it, the transaction account, the card. Beside each, write the date you last assessed it. Anything with no date is not a decision you made. Pick the one with the largest balance behind it and assess that one today, because this is realistically the only week you will.",
  reflection: "Which default did you finally assess, and how long had you been carrying it as something to think about?",
  recall: {
    q: "Leaving the United Kingdom scheme was free and easy. Why is the low opt-out rate the most informative part of the rollout?",
    a: "Because it separates two explanations for why defaults bind. If people stayed because leaving was costly, the result would tell you about the cost of exit and almost nothing about anybody's preferences. Exit was free, immediate and advertised, and on the order of one in ten used it, so what actually held people was that leaving required an act and staying required none.\n\nThe personal reading is that any exit you are offered is a right you will probably not exercise. Treat what you are placed into as what you will keep, and do the assessing in the week it is switched on, because assessing is precisely the kind of act that does not happen later."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is every arrangement I was placed into rather than chose. Rank them by what the default is costing me and tell me which one to assess this week."
},
{
  id: "defaults-forgone-match",
  track: "defaults", level: "annual",
  title: "A large minority decline free money they could withdraw the same day without penalty",
  source: "Choi, Laibson and Madrian, $100 Bills on the Sidewalk: Suboptimal Investment in 401(k) Plans (Review of Economics and Statistics, 2011)",
  gateIntro: "Your employer offers something above the legislated minimum if you contribute yourself, and you have never established whether you are capturing it, because the answer lives in a portal you dislike and a document written for lawyers. You have been at this employer three years. Whatever is on offer has been on offer for all three of them.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that establishes whether you are capturing the full employer contribution, with the date you will run it and the place you will write the answer down.",
  evidence: "United States plan administrative data restricted to a group chosen specifically to remove every reasonable excuse. The employees studied were past the age at which retirement-plan withdrawals stop attracting the early-withdrawal penalty, and their plans permitted withdrawal while still employed, so an eligible employee could contribute a dollar, collect the employer match, take the lot out the same week and be strictly better off. No horizon, no lock, no penalty. Roughly a third of the match-eligible employees in that group nonetheless left part of the match unclaimed, and the average amount forgone ran to something on the order of one to two percent of salary a year. Several firms rather than one, but only firms whose plans allow in-service withdrawal, a sample deliberately unrepresentative because it is built to be the cleanest possible case, and administrative data that cannot see whether any individual understood the rules.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "If a third of people decline money that is free, immediate and unlocked, then whatever stops people collecting an employer contribution is not the lock-up, the risk or the arithmetic.",
  why: "This study exists to kill the reasonable explanations. Every time somebody points out that people forgo an employer match, the reply is that the money is locked away for decades, or that the person is liquidity constrained, or that they have correctly worked out they need the cash now. So the authors went looking for the case where none of that applies: employees old enough that withdrawal carries no penalty, in plans that let you withdraw while still employed. Contribute, collect, withdraw, same week.\n\nRoughly a third still left part of it behind. What survives as an explanation is not a preference and not a calculation. It is that collecting requires an act, the act has no deadline, and an act with no deadline loses to everything that has one.\n\nThe size is what makes it uncomfortable. One to two percent of salary a year is roughly the magnitude people agonise over when comparing fees between funds, and here it is being declined outright by people with every incentive and no obstacle. That tells you where to look first in your own arrangements. Not at returns, not at fees, but at whether something is being offered to you that you have simply never gone and collected.",
  failureMode: "Three years at an employer that contributes above the minimum if you contribute yourself, at an arrangement you have never confirmed you are receiving in full. If the gap is one percent of a hundred and forty thousand dollar salary, that is roughly four thousand two hundred dollars of somebody else's money declined over the three years, and the reason is that the answer lives behind a portal you find unpleasant.",
  worked: "The instruction is annual, and it is written to terminate in a written answer rather than in an intention:\n\n\"In the first week of July each year, find the exact employer contribution arrangement I am on, work out whether my own contributions capture all of it, and write the answer in the same note as last year.\"\n\nThe same note is the part that matters. An annual check with nowhere to record the result becomes an annual check you cannot remember doing, so it gets skipped, or done twice, and either way produces nothing.\n\nIf you find a shortfall, the follow-up is not a resolution to contribute more. It is: \"Raise my contribution to the exact level that captures the full employer amount, effective next pay cycle, and change nothing else.\"",
  experiment: "Today, find the document or portal page that states your employer contribution arrangement in full, including anything above the legislated minimum and anything conditional on contributions of your own. Write down what it says and what you are currently contributing, side by side. If there is a gap, close it this week, because a gap that has stayed open for three years will stay open for a fourth without a dated act.",
  reflection: "Was anything on offer that you were not collecting, and how long had it been sitting there?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I be able to state my exact employer contribution arrangement from memory in thirty days?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why did the authors restrict the sample to older employees in plans that allow withdrawal while still employed?",
    a: "To strip out every defensible reason for forgoing an employer match. For those employees the money was not locked away and carried no early-withdrawal penalty, so they could contribute, collect the match and withdraw it immediately. Horizon, risk, liquidity constraint and the argument that the person needs the cash today all stop working as explanations.\n\nRoughly a third still left part of the match unclaimed, averaging on the order of one to two percent of salary a year. What remains is that collecting requires an act with no deadline, and acts with no deadline lose to everything that has one."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my employment arrangement and what I currently contribute. Work out whether I am capturing everything my employer is offering, and tell me what any shortfall has cost me so far."
},
{
  id: "defaults-simplification",
  track: "defaults", level: "standing",
  title: "Cutting the number of decisions in a form raises take-up more than explaining why the form matters",
  source: "Beshears, Choi, Laibson and Madrian, Simplification and saving (Journal of Economic Behavior and Organization)",
  gateIntro: "You sat down on Sunday to sort out where a regular investment should go. Four hours later there are eleven browser tabs open, a comparison spreadsheet with two rows filled in, and no transfer. The obstacle was never whether to invest. It was picking the allocation, and then the platform, and then whether to run it monthly or fortnightly.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction with every optional decision stripped out of it, so that what remains is an amount, a date and a destination and nothing else.",
  evidence: "A firm intervention rather than a lecture. Employees were offered a simplified enrolment card that reduced joining to a single tick: accept a pre-set contribution rate and a pre-set investment option, or do nothing. The alternative was the ordinary path, which required choosing a rate and an allocation from a menu. Take-up among employees offered the simplified card rose substantially against the standard path over a short window. Small samples in a small number of firms, short follow-up, and a design that cannot separate the effect of having fewer decisions from the effect of being handed one particular pre-set rate. Nothing in it establishes that the rate people accepted was any good for them, which is the same limitation the whole defaults literature carries.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "What stands between you and an install is usually the number of choices in the way rather than any doubt about doing it, so the fix is to delete choices rather than to resolve them.",
  why: "Two interventions are available when somebody is not doing a thing they intend to do. You can raise the motivation, or you can cut the number of decisions the act requires. The simplification result says the second is doing far more work than its reputation suggests, because it removes the real obstacle, which is that a form with four choices in it offers four separate opportunities to stop.\n\nThis bites harder than it should because every one of the choices is defensible. Which rate, which fund, which platform, monthly or fortnightly: none of those is a silly question and each deserves a little thought. But the cost of a decision is not the quality of the answer, it is the probability that the sequence finishes, and each extra choice multiplies in another number below one. Four sensible questions can turn a fifteen-minute task into a permanently unfinished one.\n\nSo the move is to pre-set everything you can and leave exactly one thing live. That is uncomfortable, because pre-setting means accepting figures you have not optimised. It is the trade the evidence supports: the loss from a mediocre pre-set figure is small and bounded, and the loss from a form you never finish is the whole thing.",
  failureMode: "Four hours on a Sunday, eleven tabs, a half-built comparison spreadsheet and no money moved, repeated three months later. The cost is not the difference between the platforms you were comparing, which is a few basis points. It is a year of contributions that did not happen while you were choosing between them.",
  worked: "Write the instruction with exactly one blank in it, and fill that blank with a figure you can live with rather than the best figure:\n\n\"Transfer $500 the day after each pay into the savings account I already have, starting this cycle.\"\n\nEverything that could have been a decision has been pre-set: the account I already have rather than a better one, the day after pay rather than a chosen date, this cycle rather than after the research. The only live variable is the amount, and it is set to something survivable rather than optimal.\n\nIf you want the allocation question answered properly, make it a separate task with its own date. It is not allowed to block this one, because the version of you who will do that research is the same version who has not done it for four months.",
  experiment: "Take whatever you have been meaning to set up and write it as a single sentence. Count the decisions still embedded in it. For every decision but one, pick the boring option right now, without research: the account you already have, the day after pay, the amount you know clears. Then execute the sentence. You should be finished inside ten minutes, and if you are not, there was still more than one live decision in it.",
  reflection: "How many separate choices had been holding up the thing you finally set up today?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does removing choices from an enrolment form beat explaining the benefits of enrolling?",
    a: "Because the obstacle is not doubt about the benefit, it is the number of points at which the sequence can stop. Every extra choice is another opportunity to defer, and the chance of finishing is a product of terms each below one, so four defensible questions can convert a short task into one that never completes.\n\nIn the simplification study, employees offered a card that reduced joining to accepting a pre-set rate and a pre-set option took it up substantially more than those sent down the ordinary path of choosing a rate and an allocation. What it does not show is that the pre-set rate was any good for them, which is the price of the design."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the thing I have been trying to set up and every decision it seems to require. Strip it back to one decision, pick the boring option for the rest, and give me the sentence to execute."
},
{
  id: "defaults-social-influence",
  track: "defaults", level: "judgement",
  title: "Who you work alongside really does change whether you enrol, and it changes it by a few percentage points",
  source: "Duflo and Saez, The Role of Information and Social Interactions in Retirement Plan Decisions: Evidence from a Randomized Experiment (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2003)",
  gateIntro: "Three people on your team have spent the week talking about the fund they moved to and the fee they are now paying. You have not moved. You are also aware that the only thing which changed this week is that three people mentioned it, and that nothing about your own arrangement changed at all, and that this bothers you slightly.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction you would set up if nobody at work had mentioned anything, then decide whether it is the same instruction.",
  evidence: "A randomised encouragement design at a large United States university. A random subset of departments was picked, and within those departments a random subset of employees was offered a small payment to attend a benefits information fair. That yields two comparisons: treated individuals against untreated individuals, and untreated colleagues inside treated departments against employees in untreated departments. Attendance rose sharply among those offered the payment, and enrolment in the supplementary retirement plan rose in the treated departments for treated and untreated employees alike, which is the peer effect. The enrolment effects were detectable and small in absolute terms, on the order of a few percentage points, and the outcome measured is enrolment rather than any amount saved. One institution, one plan, one fair, and an unusually educated workforce.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Social influence on financial behaviour is real, randomly assigned and genuinely causal, and it is also small enough that building anything around it is a mistake.",
  why: "The design is why this paper matters. Almost every claim about peers and money is observational, and observational peer effects are close to unidentifiable, because people who resemble one another choose to be near one another. Randomising which departments were encouraged, and then which people inside them, separates the influence of colleagues from the fact that colleagues are alike. What survives that is a causal object rather than a correlation.\n\nAnd it is a few percentage points. That is the number to keep hold of. The result gets cited as though it licenses building interventions, communities and product features on social proof, and what it actually supports is that a colleague mentioning something shifts enrolment slightly. Put it beside the enrolment framing results from the same literature, which move participation by tens of points, and the ordering is not close.\n\nWhat that leaves you with is a judgement rather than an install, and it cuts both ways. Being tipped off by a colleague is a perfectly good way to learn something useful, so do not dismiss the tip. But the force operating on you is not proportional to the quality of the argument, and the channel that delivers a good fund delivers a bad one with identical strength. The test is whether you would write the same instruction had nobody said anything.",
  failureMode: "You move funds because three people at work moved funds, you spend a Saturday on it, and the new fund is perfectly fine. Six months later somebody mentions a different one. The cost is not the fund, which was never the binding term. It is that the thing setting your allocation is who you had lunch with, and that mechanism has no reason to point anywhere good.",
  worked: "This one produces a filter rather than a transfer:\n\n\"Before acting on anything somebody told me, I write the instruction down, put a date seven days out on it, and only set it up if I still want it on that date without re-reading the conversation.\"\n\nThe seven days are not there so you can do research. They are there because the social signal decays and a reason does not, so whatever is still standing after a week is standing on its own.\n\nThe clause about not re-reading the conversation is the load-bearing one. Going back to the thread restarts the influence, and then you have measured the same thing twice instead of waiting it out.",
  experiment: "Take the last money change you made, or nearly made, and answer honestly where you first heard about it. If the answer is a colleague, a group chat or a podcast, that is not disqualifying, but write out what the case for it would be with the source deleted. If you cannot reconstruct a case, you have found something that arrived by influence rather than by argument, and you now know which of your arrangements to look at first.",
  reflection: "What was the last financial change you made because somebody near you had made it?",
  recall: {
    q: "Duflo and Saez identified a genuinely causal peer effect on retirement plan enrolment. Why is that a reason to be more sceptical of social proof rather than less?",
    a: "Because the randomisation that makes the effect credible makes the size credible too, and the size is a few percentage points. Randomly encouraging some departments, and some individuals within them, separated the influence of colleagues from the fact that colleagues resemble each other, so what is left is causal. It is also small.\n\nSet that beside the enrolment framing results from the same literature, which move participation by tens of percentage points, and the ordering is clear. Social influence is a real channel, worth taking a tip through and not worth designing around, and it carries a bad idea with exactly the force it carries a good one."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the money decisions I have made in the last two years and where I first heard about each of them. Tell me which ones I would still hold if the source were deleted."
},
{
  id: "defaults-investor-autonomy",
  track: "defaults", level: "annual",
  title: "Strip the labels off and most people prefer somebody else's average portfolio to the one they built",
  source: "Benartzi and Thaler, How Much Is Investor Autonomy Worth? (Journal of Finance, 2002)",
  gateIntro: "You spent a weekend building the investment mix inside your fund, you were pleased with it, and at the time you could have defended every line. That was four years ago. You could not now state the weights without logging in, you have never rebalanced, and part of the reason is that you would first have to remember why you picked them.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the annual instruction that would tell you whether the mix you built still beats the default you turned down, in a form you could actually run in twenty minutes.",
  evidence: "A survey and choice experiment rather than administrative data. Participants in retirement plans built a portfolio from the options available to them, and were then shown the projected outcome distributions of three portfolios without being told which was which: their own, the average portfolio of their colleagues, and the plan's default. A majority preferred one of the other two to the portfolio they had built. The design is both the strength and the limit. It is clean, because the labels are gone and the preference is expressed against the same kind of information in every case. But it is a stated preference over projected distributions with nothing at stake, in one set of plans, with no realised outcome measured, and it establishes nothing about whether the average portfolio actually did better.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "The portfolio you built for yourself is not visibly better than the one you would have been handed, and you cannot pick it out once your name has been taken off it.",
  why: "The experiment removes the single thing that makes your own allocation feel superior, which is that it is yours. Shown the same three projected distributions unlabelled, most participants did not choose their own. That is not a claim that the average portfolio was better. It is a claim that whatever made your portfolio feel better was not present in the outcome distribution, which is exactly where the value was supposed to be.\n\nThat matters for defaults more than it first appears, because it prices what you are giving up. The standard objection to accepting a default allocation is that it is generic while yours is tailored. If, with the label stripped off, you cannot recognise your own tailoring as an improvement, then the cost of accepting a good default is close to nothing while the cost of the weekend you spent tailoring is real and already paid.\n\nBut the result is sold far harder than it can carry. It is a stated preference over projections with no money at stake, in one setting, and it never measures which portfolio actually did better. It does not show that defaults beat choices. It shows that the confidence you feel about your own allocation is not tracking anything you can identify blind, which is narrower and considerably more useful to know about yourself.",
  failureMode: "A weekend spent building an allocation four years ago, no rebalance since, and no ability to state the weights without logging in. Whatever the tailoring was worth, it has been decaying since the Sunday you finished it, while the default option you turned down has been rebalanced the whole time by somebody whose job that is.",
  worked: "The annual instruction is a blind comparison, which is the only version of this that works:\n\n\"Every July, before looking at my own allocation, write down the allocation I would choose today from scratch. Then open both the current one and the fund's default option. If my written answer sits closer to the default than to what I am running, switch to the default.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any performance comparison. You are not checking whether your mix beat the default last year, because one year of returns carries no information and will simply make you keep whichever is currently ahead.\n\nThe order of operations is the whole mechanism. Writing your answer before you look is what removes the label. Looking first turns the exercise into a defence of what you already own.",
  experiment: "Write down, from memory and before logging in, what you believe your current investment mix is. Then log in and check. The gap between those two measures how actively the tailoring you did is being maintained. If you were more than slightly wrong, the honest question is not how to improve the allocation. It is whether you should be running a default that somebody else rebalances for you.",
  reflection: "How close was your memory of your allocation to the real one?",
  recall: {
    q: "Most of Benartzi and Thaler's participants did not choose their own portfolio once the labels were removed. What does that establish, and what does it not?",
    a: "It establishes that whatever made their own portfolio feel better was not present in the projected outcome distribution, since shown the same three distributions unlabelled, most preferred either their colleagues' average portfolio or the plan default. The confidence people have in their own allocation is therefore not tracking anything they can identify blind.\n\nIt does not establish that defaults outperform self-selected portfolios. It is a stated preference over projections with nothing at stake, in one set of plans, and no realised outcome was measured. The usable consequence is narrower: the cost of accepting a good default is small, and the cost of the weekend spent tailoring is real and already paid."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the allocation I built and here is the default option my fund offers. Tell me what my tailoring is actually buying, in basis points and in maintenance I have to keep doing."
},
{
  id: "defaults-stapling",
  track: "defaults", level: "annual",
  title: "Since November 2021 a new employer follows the fund you already have instead of opening you another one",
  source: "The Your Future, Your Super stapling rules in force from 1 November 2021, and MySuper as the legislated default product under the Stronger Super reforms",
  gateIntro: "You start somewhere new in three weeks and the onboarding pack contains the usual superannuation choice form. At your last two jobs you left that form blank, which is roughly how you came to have four accounts, three sets of administration fees and two insurance premiums you never selected. The form is sitting in front of you again.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that settles, before your first day, which single fund every future employer will pay into.",
  evidence: "Statute and delegated rules rather than a finding. Under the Your Future, Your Super reforms, from 1 November 2021 an employer taking on a new employee who does not choose a fund must ask the Australian Taxation Office whether that employee has an existing stapled fund and pay into it if there is one, rather than into the employer's own default. Where there is no staple and no choice, the default contribution must go to a MySuper-authorised product, the legislated default product introduced by the Stronger Super reforms: a single diversified or lifecycle strategy with constraints on fees and features, subject to an annual performance test administered by APRA under which a product failing two consecutive tests is closed to new members. All of it is legislation and rules made under it, so all of it can be amended, and the performance test in particular has been argued over on the question of what it actually measures.",
  replication: "statute",
  asAt: "2026-09-02",
  sourceUrl: "https://www.ato.gov.au/businesses-and-organisations/super-for-employers",
  verifiedOn: "2026-09-02",
  cheat: "Since 1 November 2021 a new employer must pay into your existing stapled fund unless you choose otherwise, so check the staple points at a fund you would pick.",
  idea: "The default that used to open you a new account at every job now follows the account you already have, which turns doing nothing from a way of accumulating accounts into a way of keeping one.",
  why: "Before stapling, the passive path had exactly the defect this whole track warns about. Not filling in the form at a new job did not mean keeping your arrangement, it meant acquiring another one, so the person who never acted collected an account per employer, each with its own administration fee and very often its own default insurance premium chewing on a small balance. Inertia was punished rather than rewarded, which is the worst possible orientation for a default given how many people are relying on it.\n\nStapling reverses the sign without touching the mechanism. Inaction now means the money follows the fund you already hold. Same inertia, opposite outcome, which is about as clean a demonstration as you will get that a default is neither good nor bad in itself. It is good when the passive path lands you somewhere you would have chosen.\n\nWhich is exactly why the reform hands you a job rather than removing one. The staple points at a fund, and which fund it points at was very likely settled by a first employer years ago. One unexamined account is a strict improvement on four unexamined accounts and it is still an unexamined account. The remaining work is to be deliberate about what sits on the other end of the staple, because from here every future employer follows it without asking anybody, including you.",
  failureMode: "Four accounts from four employers, three of them charging an administration fee and a default insurance premium against balances too small to notice. Nine years of that is a real number and none of it needed a bad decision. It needed a blank form, four times, in an era when a blank form meant a new account.",
  worked: "One instruction, and it is about the destination rather than the count:\n\n\"Once, this month, find which fund my staple currently points at, and decide whether it is the fund I would choose today, on its fees, its default investment option and its performance test record.\"\n\nAnd the clause that fixes the sequence, which is the part people get backwards:\n\n\"If it is not that fund, I choose the one I want first and consolidate into it second. Never the other way round.\"\n\nThat order matters because the staple is durable by design. Consolidating into whichever account happens to have the largest balance staples you to a fund selected by an employer you no longer work for, and every future employer then follows it without asking. Counting your accounts is a separate job with its own entry; this one is only about what sits on the far end of the staple.",
  experiment: "Find which fund is currently stapled to you in the ATO section of myGov, then spend ten minutes on that fund specifically: its fees, its default investment option, and whether it has passed the annual performance test. The question you are answering is not whether it is adequate but whether it is the one you would pick, because from here it is the account every future employer pays into by default.",
  reflection: "Which fund is stapled to you, and is it one you would choose today?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I have exactly one super account, in a fund I deliberately chose, ninety days from now?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Stapling did not change how many people fill in the superannuation choice form. Why does it change the outcome so much?",
    a: "Because it changed what the blank form does. Before the reform, not choosing at a new job meant being placed in that employer's default, so the passive path opened an account per employer, each with an administration fee and usually a default insurance premium against a small balance. Inaction accumulated accounts. Since 1 November 2021 the employer must ask the ATO for an existing stapled fund and pay into that instead, so the same inaction now keeps one.\n\nThe mechanism did not change at all. Only the direction the passive path points did, which is the general lesson: a default is good when doing nothing lands you somewhere you would have chosen. What it leaves to you is which fund the staple points at, because that was probably set by an employer years ago and every future employer will now follow it."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the super accounts in my name and the fund currently stapled to me. Tell me which one to consolidate into, and what the others have cost me in fees and insurance so far."
},
{
  id: "defaults-when-a-default-is-wrong",
  track: "defaults", level: "judgement",
  title: "A default is a bet that you are the median case, and there are three checkable places you might not be",
  source: "Named practice rather than a paper: auditing each running default against the population it was designed for, read against what the defaults literature says about its own limits",
  gateIntro: "You are thirty-eight, self-employed for two years, and roughly forty percent of what you earn arrives as irregular invoices rather than as a wage. Every piece of advice you have read this year, and every arrangement anybody has ever switched on for you, was built for a person on a fortnightly salary from one employer. You are about to copy one of them anyway.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction adapted to the income you actually receive, and name the assumption in the standard version that does not hold for you.",
  evidence: "No trial. There is no experiment that can tell an individual whether a particular default is wrong for them, and there cannot be one, because the question is about one person's circumstances rather than about an average treatment effect. What the literature supplies is the raw material for the judgement: defaults are set for the median participant, they anchor rather than merely start people, and the same studies that show how powerfully they work show that they work just as powerfully in the wrong direction. Everything past that point in this entry is craft. It is a way of organising the question, tested by nothing, and it has no effect size behind it.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Every default encodes a picture of the person it was built for, and it is only as good as the match between that picture and you.",
  why: "A default is a compressed answer to a question about a population. Somebody chose it by asking what would be least bad for the median member of a group, and the compression is the whole reason it works, because it removes a decision from millions of people at once. The price of the compression is that the answer is wrong for everybody out at the tails, and nothing in the mechanism tells you which end you are at.\n\nThere are three ways to be at a tail and they are worth naming because they are checkable. The first is the shape of your income: nearly every savings default assumes a regular wage from a single employer, so if what you receive is lumpy, seasonal or largely not a wage, the amount and the timing are both resting on an assumption that does not hold for you. The second is horizon: default investment options are built for a working life ending at a conventional age, and a materially shorter or longer horizon changes the right answer, as does a near-term claim on the money such as a deposit. The third is what already sits on your balance sheet: an arrangement that suits somebody with no debt is a different proposition for somebody carrying a mortgage at a rate above what the arrangement is expected to earn, and a different one again for somebody with no buffer at all.\n\nThe test is not whether the default is good. It is whether you can state the case for it in your own numbers. If you can, keep it and stop revisiting it. If you cannot, you are not running a considered decision, you are running somebody else's median, and the fact that a careful process produced it for them does not make it an answer for you.\n\nWhat none of this licenses is treating yourself as exceptional as a matter of habit. Most people are near the median on most dimensions, most reasons for deviating turn out on inspection to be a preference dressed up as a circumstance, and the cost of wrongly deciding you are a special case is that you take on a decision you will then never maintain. Three dimensions, answered honestly, and then back to the instruction.",
  failureMode: "Two years of a standing transfer sized to a fortnightly salary you no longer receive, dishonoured four times across eighteen months in the quiet quarters, each failure costing a fee and a little of your confidence in the arrangement, until you switched it off in April. The instruction was fine. It was written for somebody with a different income shape.",
  worked: "The instruction here is a written audit rather than a transfer, and it is deliberately short:\n\n\"Once a year I write three lines: what my income shape actually is, what my horizon actually is, and what is on my balance sheet. Then I check every running default against those three, and either write down the case for it in my own numbers or change it.\"\n\nThe adapted transfer for the case above:\n\n\"Transfer thirty percent of every invoice payment on the day it clears, rather than a fixed amount on a fixed date.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent from the audit is any longer list of things to consider. Three dimensions is a number you will actually check. A comprehensive review is something you do once, thoroughly, and never again, which is strictly worse than a short one that runs every year.",
  experiment: "Write the three lines today: income shape, horizon, balance sheet. One sentence each, honest rather than aspirational. Then take the single largest default currently running on your money and try to state the case for it using nothing but those three lines. If you cannot get through it, you have found the default to change this month. If you can, write the case down beside it and stop thinking about it until next year.",
  reflection: "Which running default could you not justify from your own three lines?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why can no study tell you whether a particular default is wrong for you, and what do you use instead?",
    a: "Because a default is chosen for the median member of a population and a trial reports an average treatment effect, and neither of those is a statement about one person's circumstances. What the literature can tell you is that defaults anchor rather than merely start people, and that the force which makes a well-matched default helpful makes a badly matched one harmful by exactly as much.\n\nWhat you use instead is craft: three checkable dimensions, being the shape of your income, your horizon, and what already sits on your balance sheet, and then the test of whether you can state the case for the running default in your own numbers. If you can, keep it. If you cannot, you are running somebody else's median."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my income shape, my horizon and my balance sheet. Go through every default currently running on my money and tell me which of them were designed for somebody who is not me."
}
);
